00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
At least 34 children died when a Peshawar School roof collapsed. Pakistan's deadliest of its kind. This is Omni View Top World News, Episode 23. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. In Peshawar, in northwestern Pakistan, the roof of the private al-Qaeda school came down on Thursday. Authorities in a hospital official put the dead at 34 children and a teacher, with more than 50 other people hurt. Lady Reading Hospital spokesman Muhammad Asim said those children were between 6 and 12.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.220
the oldest 14, and that some of the wounded were critical.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.860
Emergency services spokesperson Balal Faisi said two classroom roofs fell while second

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.600
and third grade children were inside with their teachers, and that everyone trapped

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.460
had been pulled out.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.680
Faisi told the Associated Press that nearly 100 rescuers joined, and that the speed of the

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.720
evacuation saved at least 40 lives.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.880
Injured teacher Mao Wish Zaman said the school had about 1,200 students, nursery through

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.000
fifth grade, and that the collapse felt as if the sky had fallen.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.480
Peshawar police chief Saeed Ahmed said the owner, Muhammad Khan, had been arrested and

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.100
that the building was structurally weak.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.700
Police said Khan blamed this week's reigns and that the investigation is still open.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.660
Governor Faisal Kareem Kunde asked for a report on the cause and the provincial government

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.480
formed a committee.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.240
The Globe and Mail notes that collapses like this are common in Pakistan, where building

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.840
rules are often poorly enforced.

